WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.460
- I would like to walk down the red carpet

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.380
and have the interviewers talk to me,

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.380
about the choices that I made
creatively as opposed to,

00:00:15.497 --> 00:00:17.640
"Hey, you're a female."

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.367
And be like "Yeah, and?"

00:00:21.548 --> 00:00:24.131
(upbeat music)

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.660
Having been in a band,
I was recording things,

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:34.440
and they weren't coming out right.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:35.750
And that was it,

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:38.320
I wanted to be in control
of what it sounded like.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.800
- [Woman] Emily Lazar
is a mastering engineer.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.760
- In it's most basic,
mastering engineer takes

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.040
either stereo, or multi-channel audio,

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.080
and prepares it for distribution.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.260
Whether that be making it sound the best

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.160
on a piece of vinyl, on Spotify.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.450
One mastering approach doesn't always work

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:57.620
for all of those different needs.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.980
- [Woman] But she isn't
just any mastering engineer,

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:02.450
she is the first woman
to ever to be nominated

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:06.160
in the category of Best
Engineered Album Non-Classical.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:08.570
- When that first Grammy
nomination where I was up

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:11.320
for the first woman,
for album of the year,

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:14.160
I was like completely in shock.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.450
And so I started, you know, trying to look online,

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:19.110
to see if I could figure out
if that was actually true,

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:22.040
'cause it couldn't be, in my mind.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:23.350
It just couldn't be.

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:25.020
- [Woman] Mastering the mastering industry

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.470
doesn't always save you
from the inevitable.

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:30.840
- Being female and being in audio

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.680
in the first place, was a complete oddity.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.633
There's sexism, there's ageism,

00:01:35.633 --> 00:01:38.930
there's all sorts of things that happen.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:42.750
And we're in a culture now
where you can speak out.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:45.333
I was not allowed, really,
to speak out before.

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:50.520
I also had situations where I had people

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:54.290
inject me into a situation
because I was a woman

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:57.450
and because they thought
that was a cool thing

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.130
to have a woman in there.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:02.120
Which was also equally as despicable.

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.020
These things happened, and
I'm aware that they happened,

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.450
but they did not get my attention.

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:08.070
They did not suck me down.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:11.760
And I always try to
inspire, not just women,

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.380
to follow their dreams.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:17.950
I really believe that if you can see it

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.450
and make it happen, it happens.

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:22.290
And if you only see
all the negative stuff,

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:24.100
all the people who don't want women,

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:25.930
or don't treat you like
an equal or whatever,

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:29.920
then you're gonna spend your
life kind of focusing on that.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.520
And I don't have time for that,

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:32.620
I don't have room for that.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:34.530
I have wanted to make records,

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:36.153
I don't wanna talk about why I can't.

00:02:36.153 --> 00:02:38.986
(uplifting music)

